# Extract Til You Drop

Decompose every function by repeated extract-method until no further
intention-revealing extraction remains.

## When to apply

Always, when writing or modifying logic in any layer that holds it:
{{LOGIC_LAYERS}}. That is the domain and the use cases, and equally the mappers
and error classifiers inside driven adapters and the presentation models —
each is full of decisions. This is not a refactor-only skill — it governs new
code as it is written. Views get the split rule below.

Does not apply to: generated code, configuration objects, and straight-line
adapter I/O plumbing with no decision in it. A mapper that decides (enum
values, absent fields, dates, error kinds) is logic, not plumbing.

## The rule

A function does **one** thing, at **one** level of abstraction, and its name says
what that thing is. If you can name a block of its body with a phrase that is not
already the function's name, that block is a separate function.

Signals to extract, in descending strength:

1. You wrote a comment describing the next few lines. The comment is the name.
2. A block is separated by a blank line for readability. The blank line is a seam.
3. The function mixes levels: a policy decision next to string formatting, or a
   business rule next to an error conversion.
4. The body contains `and`/`then` when described out loud: "validates the input
   **and** persists it **and** notifies".
5. A conditional branch is large enough to read as its own paragraph.
6. A loop body does non-trivial work per item.
7. A boolean expression needs more than one clause to be understood — extract it
   into a named predicate.

## Workflow

1. Write the logic the direct way first — get it correct.
2. Read the body and mark every seam from the list above.
3. Extract each seam into a private function named for **what it does** (see
   Naming), not its mechanism or its caller's use.
4. Re-read the parent. It should now read as a sentence-level summary of the
   operation, with no implementation detail left.
5. Repeat on each extracted function. Extraction usually cascades one level.
6. Stop only at the stop conditions below.

## Naming

Name every function for **what it does**: its operation and the data or result
it handles. Do not name it after how a caller uses it, a surrounding workflow,
or an anticipated purpose, unless the function actually performs that behavior.
Apply the same distinction to returned data types.

Use verb phrases that describe the operation.

| Mechanism or caller's use (bad) | Operation (good) |
|---|---|
| loop over items and check | has expired item |
| do validation step 2 | reject duplicate email |
| map DTO to entity internal | to domain |
| `list_maintenance_targets` → `ProjectMaintenanceTarget` (paginated read of stored project/owner ID pairs, named for the caller's use) | `list_project_owner_pairs` → `ProjectOwnerPair` |
| handle stuff | anything — this name is always a defect |

A function that really repairs ledger state may be named `repair_project_ledger`:
the name is right because the function performs that behavior.

Before accepting a name, ask: would it still describe the same operation if
another caller reused it? If not, rename it before completing the change.

Spell the name in the language's own casing (`hasExpiredItem`,
`has_expired_item`, `HasExpiredItem`); the rule is about the words, not the
case.

A name you cannot write without `And`, `Or`, `Handle`, `Process`, `Manage`, or a
number is telling you the extraction boundary is wrong, not that naming is hard.

## Where extracted code lives

**First, check it is an extraction at all.** A seam that turns out to be a
domain rule — an "only if", "must", "cannot", "at most", "when X then Y" sitting
inside a use case, a handler, a view-model or a state holder — is not an
extraction. It is a placement fix: move it to a named pure function in the
domain, per `separation-of-concerns`, and test it there. This is the most common
finding. Everything below stays in its own layer.

{{EXTRACT_LOCATION}}

A use case over ~80 lines, or a driving adapter over ~150 lines, almost always
hides a missing extraction — and often a rule that belongs in the domain. Check
it before adding to it.

Extracted helpers stay **private to their layer**. Extraction never moves logic
across an architectural boundary — if a helper wants a dependency its layer may
not have, the boundary is wrong, not the extraction.

## Stop conditions

Stop when all of these hold:

- Every function is nameable in one intent-revealing phrase.
- No function mixes two levels of abstraction.
- No remaining block would gain a clearer name by being extracted.
- Extracting further would produce a helper used once whose name only restates
  the line it wraps (`addOne`, `getName`) — that is over-extraction, back out.

Cost boundary: a helper that exists only to satisfy a line-count rule is noise.
The test is always "does the name add meaning", never "is the function short".

## Splitting a view

A view (UI component, template) holds no logic, but it still decomposes: split
it by **nameable concern**. A section you could describe on its own, a large
conditional branch, non-trivial markup per item of a collection, or a comment
like "left column" marks a child view. Extract it with only the props it needs,
named in plain words; if no name comes, the boundary is wrong. A two-line
fragment used once with no identity of its own stays inline. Logic found while
splitting is not extracted into the view — it moves to the presentation model
or the domain.

## Writing new logic (not just refactors)

When adding a branch to an existing function, do not grow the function — extract
the branch. A function that accreted three features is the normal failure mode,
and it never announces itself; each individual addition looked small.

## Done checklist

Before finishing any logic change:

- [ ] Every comment inside a function body has become a function name, or been
      deleted as redundant.
- [ ] No function body has an unexplained blank-line block.
- [ ] Every boolean condition with 2+ clauses is a named predicate.
- [ ] Every function and returned type is named for what it does, as a verb
      phrase describing the operation — not for its caller, workflow, or
      anticipated purpose. It would still be accurate if another caller reused it.
- [ ] The top-level function reads as a summary a non-author could follow.
- [ ] Every seam that was a business rule moved to the domain, with its test.
- [ ] No helper crosses a layer boundary.
- [ ] Every view you touched is split by nameable concern, nothing over-split.
- [ ] Tests still green — extraction is behaviour-preserving, always.
